{
  "datasetFormat": "spinebones-rag-animation",
  "version": "1.0",
  "id": "hit",
  "name": "Rhino Hit Reaction",
  "description": "Take-damage recoil reaction for the Rhino humanoid warrior. The character braces at frame 2, snaps backward from the impact at frames 4 to 8 with the body rotating +18 degrees and the head +23 degrees, then recovers through frames 10 to 16 with smooth easeInOut transitions. A subtle double overshoot at frames 20 to 24 (slight forward, then counter-backward) gives the body a natural fleshy settle before returning to the ready stance at frame 33. All 10 bones are keyed at 12 positions each. The sword is carried in the right hand and recoils with the arm.",
  "category": "hit",
  "loop": false,
  "style": [
    "stylized",
    "heavy",
    "combat"
  ],
  "motionFeatures": {
    "speed": "fast",
    "energy": "high",
    "mood": "hurt",
    "cycle": false
  },
  "requiredBones": [
    "torso",
    "head",
    "leftArm",
    "rightArm",
    "leftHand",
    "rightHand",
    "leftUpperLeg",
    "rightUpperLeg",
    "weapon"
  ],
  "usage": "Use for a take-damage reaction, hit stun, kena serangan, or being struck in combat on the current Rhino rig. Duration is 33 frames at 30 FPS (1.1 seconds). The double overshoot at frames 20 to 24 (forward then backward) is intentional and gives a fleshy settle feel. Easing is mixed: easeOut for the impact snap (frames 4 to 8), easeIn for the brace (frame 2), and easeInOut for all recovery and settle phases. This is a one-shot reaction, not a loop. The sword is attached to the right hand (bone ID 3), so wrist and arm recoil carry the blade. The source rig has two bones named right_arm: ID 2 is the upper arm (parent ID 1), and ID 3 is the right hand (parent ID 2, carrying sword ID 9). Apply tracks by boneId on this rig; name-only mapping cannot distinguish these tracks. Eyes and sword retain their local transforms and inherit their parents' motion.",
